WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.864
Locking and MVCC, done.

00:00:06.864 --> 00:00:11.729
Step back.

00:00:11.729 --> 00:00:16.593
This module built three things: a definition of correct, conflict-serializability; a protocol that produces it, strict two phase locking; and the primitive underneath, the lock.

00:00:16.593 --> 00:00:21.458
Together they run every relational database.

00:00:21.458 --> 00:00:26.117
The goal in one sentence: run thousands of transactions at once, and have the result act as if each ran alone.

00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:30.777
Theory told us what correct means.

00:00:30.777 --> 00:00:35.436
Now we make it run, faster than serving transactions one at a time.

00:00:35.436 --> 00:00:38.260
Here is the whole protocol in about twenty lines.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.083
A transaction has three operations.

00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:43.907
Reed asks for a shared lock, then reeds the value.

00:00:43.907 --> 00:00:46.730
Write asks for an exclusive lock, then writes.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.553
Commit is the one that matters.

00:00:49.553 --> 00:00:52.377
It releases every lock the transaction holds, all at once.

00:00:52.377 --> 00:00:55.200
That single rule is what makes it strict.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.024
Locks pile up while the transaction runs.

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:00.847
They drop only at commit.

00:01:00.847 --> 00:01:03.671
Nothing reads a value before its writer commits.

00:01:03.671 --> 00:01:07.105
Now zoom all the way out.

00:01:07.105 --> 00:01:10.539
A real system has five pieces that cooperate.

00:01:10.539 --> 00:01:13.973
The application layer, where you write the transaction.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:17.407
The transaction manager, which coordinates it.

00:01:17.407 --> 00:01:20.841
The lock manager, which enforces two phase locking.

00:01:20.841 --> 00:01:24.275
The scheduler, which produces conflict-serializable schedules.

00:01:24.275 --> 00:01:27.709
And the recovery manager underneath, which makes durability real.

00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:31.144
Five parts.

00:01:31.144 --> 00:01:34.578
One contract.

00:01:34.578 --> 00:01:38.012
ACID.

00:01:38.012 --> 00:01:41.446
Atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability, delivered by parts you have now seen one at a time.

00:01:41.446 --> 00:01:44.880
So where do the speedups come from?

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.314
The worked microschedule went from forty nine time units to thirty one.

00:01:48.314 --> 00:01:51.748
About thirty seven percent on two transactions.

00:01:51.748 --> 00:01:55.182
Ten to a hundred times at real scale.

00:01:55.182 --> 00:01:58.616
Idle waiting becomes useful work, and the schedule still passes the correctness test.

00:01:58.616 --> 00:02:02.656
Once those five pieces exist, the same protocol stretches.

00:02:02.656 --> 00:02:06.697
It distributes.

00:02:06.697 --> 00:02:10.737
One global transfer locks an account in New York, an account in San Francisco, and an audit log in Tokyo, and the manager holds two phase locking across all three.

00:02:10.737 --> 00:02:14.777
It recovers.

00:02:14.777 --> 00:02:18.817
The recovery manager replays the write-ahead log after a crash, so committed work survives.

00:02:18.817 --> 00:02:22.858
And it scales.

00:02:22.858 --> 00:02:26.898
Stock exchanges push millions of trades a second through this exact idea.

00:02:26.898 --> 00:02:30.938
Same core, different operating point.

00:02:30.938 --> 00:02:34.653
That is a transaction system: conflict-serializability defines correct, two phase locking enforces it.

00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:38.368
But every guarantee assumed machines stay up.

00:02:38.368 --> 00:02:42.084
They do not.

00:02:42.084 --> 00:02:45.799
Next is crash recovery, protecting committed work when the power goes out.

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:49.514
Together they make ACID real.
